    "body": "Groundtruth, a startup developed through J.B. Hunt Transport Services’ venture-building partnership with Vantora, launched an artificial intelligence platform designed to automate freight billing validation and reduce invoice discrepancies across the transportation industry, the companies announced on Monday, Nov. 16.\nFounded in 2024, Groundtruth is a Nashville-based startup with fewer than 50 employees led by CEO Alex Rustioni. The company is an independent business spun out of the venture studio operated by J.B. Hunt and Vantora, formerly UP.Labs . J.B. Hunt has served as a collaborative design partner for the past two years, while Groundtruth received funding from UP.Partners, according to a company spokesperson.\nThe platform is designed to reconcile information spread across different systems, including commercial agreements, shipment records, emails and operational documents. It is aimed at carriers, freight brokers and freight forwarders.\nThe launch comes as transportation companies continue to work through the back-office challenges associated with manual invoice processing. A December 2025 survey FreightWaves conducted with OTR Solutions found that 54% of freight brokerages described their accounts payable processes as mostly automated, but only 2% reported full automation. On the accounts receivable side, just 2% reported full automation, while 43% said their processes remained partially automated with numerous manual steps.\nInvoice discrepancies are also a concern for shippers. A 2026 survey of less-than-truckload shipping professionals conducted by MyCarrier, a provider of LTL shipping software, and research firm Mastio & Company found that 67% of respondents identified disputing carrier overcharges as the most difficult or time-consuming part of their freight audit and payment process. Nearly 20% said manually comparing freight bills with bills of lading was their biggest challenge.\nGroundtruth is intended to address the validation process before an invoice reaches a customer, rather than focusing on payment processing itself. The platform brings together information from transportation and finance systems, contracts and customer communications to check whether an invoice reflects the agreed terms.\nFuel surcharges are one example of the charges that require verification. Carriers typically calculate them using a contractually agreed surcharge schedule tied to the U.S. Department of Energy’s weekly average diesel price. Applying the wrong weekly price or an incorrect surcharge schedule can result in a billing discrepancy.\n“When it finds an exception, it shows the customer experience team the evidence behind it. A person with context on that customer’s rates and agreements can then review it and decide whether to make a correction,” Rustioni said.\nGroundtruth is close to 100% deployed across J.B. Hunt’s internal departments and is already being used daily.\nThe platform is also commercially available to other carriers, brokers and freight forwarders, though the company did not identify any external customers or disclose how many invoices it has processed.\nGroundtruth uses a cost-to-serve pricing model, charging customers a few dollars for each invoice the platform impacts, according to a company spokesperson. The company said the model delivers a fourfold to sixfold return for customers but did not provide supporting calculations or disclose how many invoices it has processed.\nGroundtruth is the second startup announced through J.B. Hunt’s partnership with Vantora, The first, Overroute , launched in July with an AI-powered freight execution platform. Billing was among the problems J.B. Hunt and Vantora weighed early on, including reducing billing complexity, before they chose load execution for Overroute.\nThe partnership is focused on developing startups to address operational challenges in transportation and other essential industries. Groundtruth’s launch extends that effort into freight finance and back-office operations.\nWhy this matters:\nFreight billing errors and manual invoice reconciliation can create administrative costs, delay payments and lead to disputes between carriers, brokers and shippers. As transportation companies look to automate back-office operations, tools that validate charges against contracts, shipment records and fuel surcharge schedules could reduce manual work and improve billing accuracy.",
